00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
everyone welcome to the Commission's  restroom and to this statement by  Commissioner Malmstrom on the use  proposals ideas on WTO modernization and  also to address an issue that will be  high on the agenda of next week  United Nations General Assembly the  Global Alliance for torture free trade  the Commissioner will make a statement  and then she'll be able to answer other  questions that you might have on these  and other topics without further ado  commissioner  thank you vets thank you very much  Daniel good morning ladies and gentlemen  yes today the Commission is publishing  some of our initial ideas to reform the  WTO the World Trade Organization and  global trade rules this paper is online  with a press release and after the  midday briefing there will be a  technical briefing in this very room for  those of you who have particular  interests as you know over the past  several decades the multilateral trading  system has provided predictability and  stability for companies economies across  the world it has helped economies to  growth and lift in hundreds of millions  of people out of poverty the appellate  body a very important part of the WTO  system to resolve trade conflicts has  been one of the most successful  international bodies for settling  disputes WTO members have generally be  good at complying with its ruling but  now the WTO system is slowly grinning to  a halt it is probably in the steepest  crisis ever since the start of this  century the world has witnessed rapid  economic growth technological changes  that has transformed not only trade  routes but also the very way in which we  trade and the result has been bigger  gaps in the rules of WTO either because  rules have become outdated or because of  the absence of rules in many areas for  instance just two examples market  distorting subsidies often channeled  through state-owned enterprises they go  unchecked because the current rules  don't cover them properly or e-commerce  the rules don't cover ecommerce at all  it's a new and very important area of  trade and that leaves big chunks of the  new digital economy out  site agreed and enforceable rules and  WTO is a member driven organization so  it can't simply reform itself the  responsibility lies only within its  members another important issue I  mentioned the dispute settlement  mechanism that is the core in a WTO  ability to function in a meaningful way  and the applet body is on the verge of  paralysis right now because the  nomination of new judges to replace  those who are retiring are being blocked  by the US another example many member  countries in the WTO ignore their  transparency obligations not being open  with a trade policy when it comes to  government support for instance they  have an obligation to report that but  they don't  so the WTO is now able to function  efficiently with these behaviors and  that means we are not able to secure a  level playing field for business key  members are not supporting the system  but opening for unilateral measures with  effects that we unfortunately know too  well so the WTO must adapt or it will be  in serious trouble and everybody will be  hurt but of course the most vulnerable  countries will suffer the most and only  the members can change this organization  and that is why in the EU who depend on  fair transparent rule-based trade for  our economic prosperity want to take a  leading role in pushing for reforms here  we are publishing some ideas today we  are discussing this with member states  and European Parliament of course and we  want to start discussions with other WTO  members how we can make the system more  efficient for everybody so you will see  in these papers that we talk about how  we can update the international trade  rulebook  so it's fit for purposes for the global  economy looking into new rules or  updating old ones in several areas  tackling forced technology transfers and  industrial subsidies for instance it  looks at how we can strengthen the WTO  monitoring role to make committed  committees and trade policies poor trade  policy reviews more efficient and to  sanction WTO members if they repeatedly  fail  to meet their obligation it discusses  how we can work in in new areas without  everybody agree today a couple handful  even one member can block others from  even discussing new area so we need to  see if we can work with pleura laterals  that are open and inclusive in order to  move forward in important areas such as  e-commerce and we also look at how we  can overcome the deadlock of the dispute  settlement system making specific  proposals to address the concerns that  has led to the blocking of appointment  of judges  these improvements could improve the  dispute settlement make it more  efficient and timely and unable the  enable the judges to focus substance so  as I said we are working with member  states on this European Parliament and  also with many other parties for  instance we have a three-way talk with  the US and Japan and we will be meeting  again next week and we will also start  discussions with China one of the  outcomes of the EU China summit this  summer was to set up a working group on  on WTO issues and I just came back from  Argentina g20 trade ministerial meetings  there also there was a collective joint  statement that we do need to work to  reform and modernize the WTO so we will  continue this discussion the coming and  the coming weeks and put even more  concrete proposals on the table and let  me also take this opportunity to tell  you about an important event taking  place in New York next week on Monday  here the EU is leading the way in  efforts to stop trade in goods that can  lead to torture or death penalty so in  New York on Monday there will be the  first ministerial meeting for the  alliance of torture free trade you might  remember that last September in New York  together with Argentina and Mongolia the  EU launched this initiative with a goal  to end trade in goods used for capital  punishment or torture and over the past  few decades more and more countries  worldwide had committed themselves to  eradicate  death penalty torture and inhuman and  degrading treatment but still many  countries continue to systematically  carry out torture and the death penalty  using products that are tracked traded  and shipped internationally and this is  unacceptable and that's why we have set  up the alliance behind me you can see  some examples of the types of terrible  goods that we are trying to bet that  these products exist they can be bought  and they are images and videos also  available for on our website more than  60 countries from all over the world  have joined the alliance for torture  free trade so far and we have been  focusing on four areas working towards  controlling and restricting exports  including putting exports ban in places  like we have in in the EU second to set  up a platform for customs authorities to  monitor trade flows and identify new  products third providing technical  assistance to help countries to put  legislation in place and fourth  exchanging best practices for  enforcement and just before the summer  we had a meeting here in Brussels that I  shared to say share know-how and  resources with countries who want to  introduce tough export controls and at  the ministerial meeting in New York on  Monday we'll be looking at how we can  keep up the international momentum to  stop this trade we'll be hearing from  experts and we hope that more countries  will join the Alliance at that occasion  and importantly we will discuss how to  launch work towards a binding United  Nation Convention to ban it so this is  what I wanted to say just before ending  wild I am on the other side of the  Atlantic I am also going on Wednesday to  Montreal for the first birthday  celebrations of seta the Canadian  agreement meeting with the Canadian  Trade Minister and take stock of how the  implementation of the agreement is going  the actual anniversary is this week on  Friday there will not be any birthday  cakes here in the press room but there  will be material that we can share with  you videos  examples and statistics of how the  the EU countries are benefiting from  from this agreement as from a Friday so  that's what

